## Variant calling ##
Variant calling (or variant identification) is the process of finding positions in the genome where your samples differ from the reference genome or from 
each other.
These differences are called genetic variants.

In population genetics, the most common variants are:
Variant type                        	   Example
SNP (single nucleotide polymorphism)	    A → G
Insertion                          	extra bases added
Deletion                             	bases missing

## What variant calling means biologically ##

Suppose the reference genome has:
Position 1000 = A

You sequence three bats and align their reads.
At that position:
Bat     	Observed base
Bat 1	        A
Bat 2       	G
Bat 3       	A

This suggests there is a SNP: A/G polymorphism
Variant calling is the computational process that detects this difference from aligned sequencing data.

An A/G polymorphism means that at a particular position in the genome, some individuals have the nucleotide A while others have G.
This is a type of SNP (Single Nucleotide Polymorphism).

## Example ##
Suppose we look at position 1050 in the genome.
Reference genome: Position 1050 = A
Now imagine sequencing three bats:
Bat	    Base at position 1050
Bat 1    	A
Bat 2	    G
Bat 3	    A

Since both A and G occur in the population, this position is called an:
A/G polymorphism

Meaning:
one allele = A
another allele = G

## What “polymorphism” means ##
“Poly” = many
“morphism” = forms

So polymorphism means: a genomic position exists in multiple forms within a population.

## Genotype vs allele ##
At one SNP position:
Possible genotypes are:
Genotype	             Meaning
AA	          both chromosome copies have A
AG          	one A and one G
GG	          both copies have G

In diploid organisms like bats, each individual has two copies of the genome, so two alleles are present at each position.

## Visualization ##

Suppose the reference sequence is:
...T C A G T...
       ^
At the marked position:
Some bats may have:
...T C A G T...
Others may have:
...T C G G T...
That single base difference is the polymorphism.

## Why these polymorphisms matter ##
These SNPs are the raw material for population genetics.
By analyzing thousands of SNPs across individuals, you can study:

genetic diversity, relatedness, population structure, gene flow, species differentiation, evolutionary history

### In that example, the three bats could be either:###

three individuals of the same species
or
individuals from different species
The example was only meant to illustrate what an A/G polymorphism is.
However, in population genetics, the interpretation changes depending on the biological context.

If the bats are from the SAME species
An A/G polymorphism usually represents: normal genetic variation within the species

Example:
Individual	Genotype
Bat 1	         AA
Bat 2	         AG
Bat 3	         GG

This kind of variation is what population genetic analyses mainly study.
You can estimate: allele frequencies, heterozygosity, population structure, gene flow

If the bats are from DIFFERENT species
Then the SNP may represent: species divergence, fixed differences between species

Example:
Species         	Typical allele
Species A           	A
Species B            	G

Such variants can help identify: species boundaries, evolutionary relationships, hybridization, adaptive divergence

## Why variant calling is necessary ##

Raw sequencing reads alone are not directly useful for population genetics.

You need to identify:
which genomic positions vary
which individuals carry which alleles
allele frequencies
genotype patterns

These variants become the basis for: PCA, admixture analysis, FST, phylogenetics, diversity estimation, demographic inference

## Core idea of variant calling ##

Variant callers examine aligned reads in BAM files and ask:
“At this genomic position, what nucleotide is truly present in each sample?”

The challenge is distinguishing:
true biological variation from sequencing errors, PCR artifacts, alignment mistakes

### How variant calling works ###

Step 1: Reads are aligned
FASTQ → alignment → BAM
Now every read has genomic coordinates.

Step 2: Examine each genomic position
The variant caller scans the genome position by position.
Example:
chr1:1050
All reads covering that position are collected.

Step 3: Create a pileup
The software stacks all overlapping reads.
Example:
Reference: A
Reads:
A
A
G
A
A
G
This is called a pileup.

Step 4: Evaluate evidence
The caller considers:
   Factor	                               Why important
Number of reads supporting allele	       confidence
Base quality scores	                   sequencing accuracy
Mapping quality	                       alignment confidence
Strand balance                        	artifact detection
Depth of coverage                     	statistical power

Step 5: Infer genotype
The software estimates the genotype.
Examples:
Read pattern	    Genotype
Mostly A	          AA
Mostly G	          GG
Mix of A and G    	AG

Step 6: Write variants to a VCF file

Results are stored in a VCF file.
VCF = Variant Call Format.
Example:
#CHROM POS ID REF ALT QUAL
chr1   1050 .  A   G   99

Meaning:
reference allele = A
alternative allele = G
high confidence SNP

## Why alignment quality matters ##

Variant calling depends heavily on correct alignment.
Bad alignment can create false variants.

For example:
reads mapped to wrong locations
repetitive regions
low-quality mappings
can appear as fake SNPs.

That is why: BAM sorting, BAM indexing, mapping quality filtering, are important preprocessing steps.

